It is required to bisect it.

Take an arbitrary point {D} on {AB}. Cut off {AE} from {AC} equal to {AD}, and join {DE}. Construct the equilateral triangle {DEF} on {DE}, and join {AF}. [!just I.3; I.Post.1, I.1]

I say that the angle {BAC} is bisected by the straight line {AF}.

Since {AD} equals {AE}, and {AF} is common, therefore the two sides {AD} and {AF} equal the two sides {EA} and {AF} respectively.

And the base {DF} equals the base {EF}, therefore the angle {DAF} equals the angle {EAF}. [!just I.Def.20, I.8]

Therefore the given rectilinear angle {BAC} is bisected by the straight line {AF}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
<br clear="all">
